1 NOTE === GEDCOM Note ===
2 CONT John was born on June 9, 1971 and died on December 1, 1853. On March 3, 1816 he married Elizabeth Moore (1800-1878) daughter of Dr. DAvid and Elizabeth Essex Moore of North River. John was a balcksmit
2 CONC h by trade and resided with his family in a house of the property where the new Fire Hall is situated on Kent Street, Charlottetown. while the robinsons liver there, Masonic meetins were held in one o
2 CONC f the upstair rooms, which was kept solely for that purpose. Later as their sons gres to manhood and expressed a desite to becomre farmers, John purchased McAusland or Peter's Island, later to become 
2 CONC known as 'Robinson's Lisland,' situated north of Rustico. This little island was a gem, in that it boasted two miles of sand dunes for pasture land, a growth of soft and hard wood, cranberry bogs and 
2 CONC two hundred acres suitable for farming. It also had a house and outbuildings. Here John settled his sons Peter Wendover and John williamm and it reamined in teh family until 1937, when it was sold to 
2 CONC the Government for a National Park.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT About 1848 John robinson moved to Mount Pleasnad, Lot 12 where he became involved in shipbuilding. while on a business trip to st. Pierre, a French possession south of Newfoundland, a disastrous fire 
2 CONC brok out threatening total destruction. mr. Robinson organized a fire brigade and after strenuous labour succeeded in brining the fire under control. for this heroic deed the French government present
2 CONC ed him with a medal inscribed thus: "A JOHN ROBINSON RECOMPENSE DE SON DEVOUMENT LORS DU 11 OCTOBRE 1844 A ST PIERRE DE TERRE NEUVE". To John robinson as a reward for his great and voluntary help on t
2 CONC he occasion of the great fire as Isle St. Pierre on 11 October 1844.:
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT John died at Mount Pleasand in Lot 12 of a hernia caused by a kick from a horse. He is buried in St. James Church Cemetery, Port Hill, Prince Edward Island.
